# TRUSTFLOW — COMPLETE UI/UX SIMPLIFICATION & VISUAL OVERHAUL

## ROLE

Act as a senior product designer, UX architect, and frontend engineer specialising in premium, minimal fintech applications.

Your task is to carry out a complete visual and user-experience overhaul of the existing TrustFlow application.

This is a redesign of an existing working project, not a request to build a new application.

The objective is to make TrustFlow feel simple, polished, intuitive, and trustworthy. A first-time user should understand what the product does and how to get started without reading lengthy explanations or learning blockchain terminology.

**Do not work on backend functionality, API integrations, smart contracts, wallet architecture, or payment execution in this task.** Preserve the existing implementation and concentrate on the visual experience, page structure, navigation, responsive behaviour, and frontend presentation.

---

# 1. PRODUCT POSITIONING

TrustFlow is a protected payment platform.

Its primary audience is:

- Freelancers who work with international clients and need a reliable way to receive payment.
- Clients and individuals who want to pay for services without worrying about paying before work is delivered.
- Everyday people who want to make purchases or payments under agreed conditions.

However, the primary positioning and landing-page messaging must focus on freelancers and their clients.

The central problem is simple:

People want to work together, but neither party wants to take unnecessary payment risks.

TrustFlow helps both parties agree on the work, reserve the payment, and release the money when the agreed work is approved.

### Communication rules

Use ordinary, everyday language.

Avoid:
- Blockchain terminology.
- Smart contract terminology.
- Escrow protocol terminology.
- Technical explanations of how funds are locked.
- AI-generated marketing clichés.
- Excessive descriptions of security architecture.
- Repetitive statements explaining the same benefit.
- Unnecessary emojis and decorative symbols.

Never assume users understand how escrow works.

Explain the product through familiar actions: agree, pay, deliver, approve.

The interface should communicate confidence without overwhelming users with information.

